# Metal–Nitrogen–Carbon Single‐Atom Aerogels as Self‐Supporting Electrodes for Dechlorination of 1,2‐Dichloroethane

## abstract
Single‐atom catalysts (SACs) have received widespread interest for their high atomic efficiency, enriched active sites, excellent catalytic performance, and low cost. However, the agglomeration of single metal atoms and the use of inactive additives for affixing powdery SACs on planar electrodes may reduce the density of active sites, diminish the charge transport to active sites, and thus suppress their performance. Herein, a series of metal–nitrogen–carbon single‐atom aerogels (M‐SAAs, M: Cu, Ni, Au, Ru) are synthesized via a universal strategy, in which the merits of metal organic frameworks and carbon aerogels are perfectly combined to prevent the agglomeration of single metal atoms and overcome the problem of poor electrical conductivity. The as‐prepared M‐SAAs can be directly employed as self‐supporting electrodes for the electrochemical dechlorination of 1,2‐dichloroethane, and outstanding activity and stability are observed. Significantly, the Cu‐SAA with abundant Cu−N4 sites shows an extraordinarily high ethylene production rate of 446 µmol h−1, with a selectivity of 99% and Faradaic efficiency of 64%. Moreover, theoretical calculations are performed to demonstrate the selectivity and activity of different metal active sites. This study provides a new strategy to exploit highly effective SACs and offers an intensive insight into the mechanism of electrochemical dechlorination reactions.

Single-atom catalysts (SACs) have received widespread interest for their high atomic efficiency, enriched active sites, excellent catalytic performance, and low cost. However, the agglomeration of single metal atoms and the use of inactive additives for affixing powdery SACs on planar electrodes may reduce the density of active sites, diminish the charge transport to active sites, and thus suppress their performance. Herein, a series of metal–nitrogen–carbon single-atom aerogels (M-SAAs, M: Cu, Ni, Au, Ru) are synthesized via a universal strategy, in which the merits of metal organic frameworks and carbon aerogels are perfectly combined to prevent the agglomeration of single metal atoms and overcome the problem of poor electrical conductivity. The as-prepared M-SAAs can be directly employed as self-supporting electrodes for the electrochemical dechlorination of 1,2-dichloroethane, and outstanding activity and stability are observed. Significantly, the $\mathsf{c}_{\mathsf{u}}$ -SAA with abundant $C u-N_{4}$ sites shows an extraordinarily high ethylene production rate of $446\upmu\mathrm{molh}^{-1}$ , with a selectivity of $99\%$ and Faradaic efficiency of $64\%$ . Moreover, theoretical calculations are performed to demonstrate the selectivity and activity of different metal active sites. This work provides a new strategy to exploit highly effective SACs and offers an intensive insight into the mechanism of electrochemical dechlorination reactions.
## 1. Introduction
Chlorinated organic compounds (COCs) are usually harmful to the environment and human health because of their persistent, bio-accumulative, and toxic nature.[1] Recently, various dechlorination techniques have been developed to remediate COCs to non-toxic materials, e.g., chemical catalysis, photocatalysis, biological reduction, and electrochemical reduction.[2,3] Among them, electrochemical reductive dechlorination has been illustrated to be a feasible approach to transform chlorinated organic pollutants to decomposable organics or high value-added industrial raw materials at room temperature and ambient pressure.[4,5] In addition, electrochemical dechlorination reaction (EDR) is an eco-friendly process without the secondary pollution induced.[6] For instance, 1,2-dichloroethane (DCE) can be directly transformed by EDR into ethylene and vinyl chloride.[7] However, because of the high stability of C−Cl bond (bond energy: 327  kJ  mol−1) and complex dechlorination mechanism, high-efficiency electrocatalysts, e.g., Pd and $\operatorname{Ag}$ are required to improve the efficiency and selectivity of EDR, and the practical application of electrochemical dechlorination technology is seriously restricted by the needs of such high-cost noble-metal electrocatalysts. Thus, the development of new, cost-effective electrocatalysts is a key for the further advance of EDR technology.
Recently, a variety of non-noble metal materials have been applied for EDR, e.g., Cu, Ni, $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , carbon and composite materials.[8–12] In particular, single-atom catalysts (SACs) have attracted a great deal of interest due to their high atomic efficiency, low cost and excellent dechlorination activities.[13,14] For example, it has been demonstrated that the 2D iron–nitrogen– carbon SACs deliver a decent performance for reductive dechlorination of DCE.[15,16] And Xu et al. also revealed that atomically dispersed Ni on N-doped graphene was efficient for EDR of chloroacetic acids.[17] However, single metal atoms in SACs are tended to agglomerate to form nanoclusters or nanoparticles in the preparation and application processes, which reduces the active sites and deteriorates the catalytic performance.[18,19] Moreover, the powdery SACs need to be physically affixed on the planar carbon electrode with inactive additives, leading to limited active sites and poor electrical conductivity.[20,21] Therefore, rational design of the SACs electrode structure to boost effective active catalytic sites with enhanced electrical conductivity and structural stability is of great significant to further improve dechlorination performance of the SACs.
The stability of metal–nitrogen–carbon (M−N−C) SACs is mainly influenced by the coordination environment, relative density, and interactions of single metal atoms with carbon supports.[22] Carbon aerogels (CAs) are known for their unique features of 3D macroscopic structure, low apparent density, abundant pore structure, large specific surface area, high conductivity, good chemical stability, environmental compatibility, and so on, which makes them a promising host for preparing SACs with high stability and reactivity.[23–26] First, the well-distributed 3D porous architecture and low apparent density can effectively hinder the agglomeration of metal atoms.[27,28] Meanwhile, the abundant pore structure and large specific surface area are beneficial to increase the amount of active sites and maximize their exposure.[29,30] In addition, the self-supported properties and high conductivity can also enhance the mass/ electron transfer efficiency, overcoming the problems caused by the use of inactive binders.[31,32] Therefore, the construction of metal–nitrogen–carbon single-atom aerogels (M-SAAs) represent a promising research area with a bright prospect.
In this work, a universal approach by using metal organic frameworks (MOF) as self-template and adopting directional freeze-casting strategy is developed for the preparation of a series of M-SAAs (M: Cu, Ni, Au, and Ru) with 3D-ordered hierarchical porous structure. And different M-SAAs were successfully obtained just by varying the metal precursors. In this approach, the structure advantages of metal–organic frameworks and carbon aerogels were perfectly combined. And the strategy could not only prevent the agglomeration of single metal atoms, but also overcome the problem of poor electrical conductivity caused by the binder. The as-prepared M-SAAs were employed as self-supporting electrodes for electrochemical dechlorination reaction of DCE (DCE-EDR), and all of them exhibited excellent electrochemical performance. Significantly, $\mathrm{Cu-SAA}$ with abundant $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ sites shows the highest ethylene production rate (PR, $446\upmu\mathrm{molh}^{-1})$ and Faradaic efficiency (FE, $64\%$ reported thus far, and an excellent selectivity of $99\%$ . The theoretical calculations demonstrated that $\mathrm{M}{-}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ sites were mainly conducive to the production of ethylene, while $\mathrm{M}{\mathrm{-N}_{3}}$ sites led to the formation of vinyl chloride; and among them, $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}{-}\mathrm{N}_{3}$ sites show the lowest energy barrier for ethylene and vinyl chloride, respectively. This work provides a new universal approach to prepare stable and high-efficiency SACs for the electrocatalytic applications, and the superior performance of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -SAA electrochemical dechlorination demonstrates the potential of this approach for developing new electrocatalysts for the advance of EDR techniques.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Preparation and Characterization of M-SAAs
The processes for preparing M-SAAs are illustrated schematically in Figure 1a. Typically, the mixed suspension of graphene oxide (GO), cellulose, and metal-based zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (M-ZIF-8) is directionally frozen and freeze-dried. And then after further pyrolyzing and leaching, the M-SAAs with 3D-ordered hierarchical porous structure and excellent mechanical property are obtained. In the preparation process, GO and cellulose are employed as versatile building blocks to construct the carbon aerogel supports, where the use of cellulose is favorable for improving the cross-linking of GO nanosheets.[33,34] The M-ZIF-8 plays multiple roles. First, it serves as the precursor of metal and nitrogen, in which metal atoms (Cu, Ni, Au, and Ru) are embedded as mixed metal nodes or trapped in pores.[35,36] Meanwhile, ZIF-8 offers a protective fence to avoid aggregation of metal atoms. In the carbonization process, Zn nodes are evaporated, leaving the skeleton with abundant N-rich defects; and the adjacent metal cations are coordinated and stabilized by $\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms, forming the M−N−C single-atom structure. Moreover, ZIF-8 acts as a template to introduce a large number of pores into aerogels during the pyrolysis process. Together with the directional freeze-casting, the 3D-ordered hierarchical porous structure is constructed into M-SAAs.[37,38] For comparison, CAs were also prepared by following the same process except the use of ZIF-8.
As show in Figure  1b–d, Cu-SAA can almost recover to its original dimension when it is compressed from ${\approx}1.0$ to $0.2\mathrm{cm}$ $20\%$ of its original height), suggesting a decent elasticity and mechanical strength, which meet the requirements of self-supporting electrode.
The elasticity of M-SAAs is mainly due to the formation of highly cross-linked structure of GO, cellulose, and ZIF-8. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) observations in Figure 1e–g and Figure S1 (Supporting Information) show that there are abundant ordered pores with the size from several to tens microns formed by the interconnection of folded carbon nanosheets (bottom view). These large pores are created by the vertically grown ice crystals in the process of directional freeze-casting. And from the side view it can be observed that the channels are arranged neatly, which further support the formation mechanism of the pores. These channels could significantly facilitate the transfer efficiency of reactants and products in the reaction process. Moreover, the isotherms and pore size distributions from $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption measurements clearly demonstrate the hierarchical pore structure of M-SAAs (Figure S2 and Table S1, Supporting Information). In addition, the specific surface areas of CA, Cu-SAA, Ni-SAA, Au-SAA, and Ru-SAA are 590, 543, 541, 564, and $582\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Such a 3D-ordered hierarchical porous structure with a large specific surface area significantly improve the exposure of single metal atoms and improve the mass/electron transfer efficiency of M-SAAs.
The mass loadings of Cu, Ni, Au, and Ru in Cu-SAA, Ni-SAA, Au-SAA, and Ru-SAA are detected by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) to be $0.49\%$ $0.66\%$ , $1.08\%$ , and $0.50\%$ , respectively. However, transmission electron microscopy (TEM) observations reveal that M-SAAs are composed of clear carbon nanosheets without the formation of metal nanoparticles, as verified by the representative images collected from Cu-SAA (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Correspondingly, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns and Raman spectra of M-SAAs (Figure S4, Supporting Information) show only the signals from carbon host. Moreover, due to the low metal contents, no clear peak of metal elements can be found in the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) survey spectra of M-SAAs either (Figure S5, Supporting Information). To explore the states of metal species in M-SAAs, aberrationcorrected high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) was conducted. As shown in Figure 2, plenty of well-dispersed bright dots are observed in all M-SAAs, which suggest that Cu, Ni, Au, and Ru exist in the form of monodispersed atoms on carbon nanosheets. As a representative, the element mapping images of Cu-SAA (Figure 2c) suggests a homogeneous distribution of Cu and N. Obviously, Cu and N atoms are located both on the GO and GO/Cellulose/ ZIF derived carbon, proving that metal atoms cannot be completely locked by ZIF-8 during the pyrolysis process at high temperature. The above results indicate that single-atom aerogels with 3D-ordered hierarchical porous structure and excellent mechanical property are successfully prepared by using the MOF self-templating and directional freeze-casting strategy.
## 2.2. Electrocatalytic Performances of M-SAAs
The electrocatalytic performances of M-SAAs for DCE-EDR were investigated with a three-electrode system (Figure  4a).
First, in the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves, an obvious reduction peak is observed at $-2.75\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs SCE) with the addition of $0.01\textbf{M}$ DCE for all M-SAAs (Figure $4\mathrm{b,c}$ ), while no peak can be found without DCE, suggesting that the reduction peak is corresponded to the dechlorination of DCE. Meanwhile, linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves reveal that M-SAAs delivers a much higher current than CA at the same potential, indicating that single metal atoms could significantly improve the electrochemical activity (Figure 4d). In particular, Cu-SAA exhibits the highest activity among all M-SAAs, which is further supported by its lowest Tafel slope (Figure 4e). Furthermore, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) Nyquist plots (Figure  4f;
Table S3, Supporting Information) prove that $\mathrm{Cu-SAA}$ has the lowest charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ . It is interesting to note that the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of both CA and M-SAAs $(30-56~\Omega~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ are significantly lower than that of powdered SACs $(306-318\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , fully embodying the superiority of self-supporting construction.[15,16] Furthermore, $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ and ohmic resistances $(R_{\mathrm{ohm}})$ of M-SAAs are much higher than Warburg resistances (W), proving that the reaction rate of DCE-EDR is determined by charge transfer efficiency.
The electrocatalytic performances of M-SAAs for DCE-EDR were further evaluated by potentiostatic electrolysis. Ethylene and vinyl chloride were revealed to be the dominating organic products (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the hydrogen evolution cannot be completely avoided while the use of weak proton solvent (N,N-Dimethylformamide). As shown in Figure  5a, the production rate (PR) of ethylene increased rapidly with the increase of potential, while the growth rate of ethylene selectivity decreased gradually. Therefore, combined with the results of CV, $-2.75\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs SCE) was selected for the following tests. It should be noted that ethylene PR and selectivity of all M-SAAs are higher than these of CA, demonstrating prominent EDR activity induced by single metal atoms (Figure  5b). Particularly, Cu-SAA shows the highest ethylene PR $(446\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\h^{-1}})$ and selectivity $(99\%)$ , suggesting that Cu atoms enable the formation of active sites with the highest EDR activity. This is further confirmed by the comparison of Faradaic efficiency for producing ethylene $(\mathrm{FE_{\mathrm{ethylene}}})$ under varying potentials, and a $\mathrm{FE_{\mathrm{ethylene}}}$ as high as $64\%$ is achieved by using Cu-SAA (Figure 5c). Meanwhile, the trend of FE with potential for all materials is similar to that of selectivity, further proving the optimal potential is $-2.75\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs SCE). Moreover, the effect of DCE concentration on the electrocatalytic performance were also evaluated (Figure S10, Supporting Information). The PR of ethylene increased rapidly with the increase of DCE concentration, while the growth rate of selectivity and FE of ethylene are decreased gradually. Therefore, the DCE concentration of $0.1\mathrm{~m~}$ has been chosen. Interestingly, the ethylene PR and $\mathrm{FE_{\mathrm{ethylene}}}$ of Cu-SAA are the highest reported thus far (Table S4, Supporting Information). The concentration of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ is rapidly increased as the reaction goes on, and the tendency is correlated with ethylene PR, further suggesting the successful dechlorination of DCE (Figure 5d).
The cycling experiments and long-term tests suggest a preeminent stability of M-SAAs (Figure 5e,f). And the Cu-SAA after the stability test has been further characterized by ICPOES, HAADF-STEM, XRD, and Raman. The loading amount of Cu in $\mathrm{{Cu-SAA}}$ is $0.47\%$ as detected by ICP-OES, which is basically the same as that in fresh Cu-SAA. In addition, the HAADF-STEM images (Figure  S11a,b, Supporting Information) reveal that no metal particles are formed after the stability test, and Cu still exists in the form of monodispersed atoms on nanosheets in the Cu-SAA. The result is further supported by the XRD pattern and Raman spectrum in Figure  S11c,d (Supporting Information), respectively, where only the signals for carbon nanosheets are observed. These findings demonstrate that the Cu single atoms in Cu-SAA are stable, and they maintain the initial structure after the reaction. The above results illustrate that Cu-SAA shows extraordinarily high performance and stability for DCE-EDR and the design of M−N−C singleatom aerogel is an effective approach for developing advanced self-supporting catalysts for EDR.
## 2.3. Theoretical Calculations
Theoretical calculations were systematically conducted to further understand the dechlorination mechanism of M-SAAs for DCE-EDR. XAFS measurements suggested that $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ is the dominant coordination mode, and the three-coordinate is always inevitable in the preparation process.[39,40] Therefore, considering the above analysis and the stability of $\mathrm{M}{-}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{M}\mathrm{-N}_{3}$ structures, the geometry structure of $\mathrm{Cu\mathrm{-}N_{4},}$ Cu– ${\bf N}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}},$ ${\mathrm{Ni-N}}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Au\mathrm{-N_{4},}}$ $\mathsf{A u\mathrm{-N}}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Ru-N}_{4},$ and $\mathrm{Ru}{-}\mathrm{N}_{3}$ sites were constructed (Figure  S12, Supporting Information). With regard to the dechlorination pathway for dechlorination of DCE (Figure 6a),[7,10,15,16] the energy profile for the formation of ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{CH}}_{2}$ states that breaking the first $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-Cl}$ bond is the ratedetermining step for the majority of $\mathrm{\Delta\M-N_{4}}$ sites, while the desorption of ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{CH}}_{2}$ is the rate-determining step for most of $\mathrm{M}{\mathrm{-N}_{3}}$ sites, due to the high unsaturated degree of single metal atom in $\mathrm{M}{\mathrm{-N}_{3}}$ species (Figure  6b). Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{\Delta\M-N_{4}}$ species show a relatively lower energy barrier than $\mathrm{M}{\mathrm{-N}_{3}}$ species. Particularly, $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ species have the lowest energy barrier of $1.07\mathrm{eV},$ which is in consistence with the highest activity of Cu– $\mathrm{N}_{4}$ for the production of ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{CH}}_{2}$ . On the contrary, the $\mathrm{M}{\mathrm{-N}_{3}}$ site owns lower energy barrier for the production of ${\mathrm{CHClCH}}_{2}$ than $\mathrm{M}{\mathrm{-N}_{4}}$ sites (Figure  6c), and the $\mathrm{Cu}{-}\mathrm{N}_{3}$ site shows the lowest energy barrier $(0.62~\mathrm{eV})$ for ${\mathrm{CHClCH}}_{2}$ . Therefore, the observation of the high selectivity of ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{CH}}_{2}$ by $\mathrm{Cu-SAA}$ agrees well with the dominant coordination mode of $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ in Cu-SAA, as evidenced by XANES and EXAFS results.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, we prepared a series of M–N–C single-atom aerogels including Cu, Ni, Au, Ru by a universal method through directional freezing the mixed suspension of GO, cellulose, and M-ZIF-8, followed by pyrolyzing. The fence effect of ZIF-8 and well-designed 3D-ordered hierarchical porous architecture of aerogel drastically hinders the agglomeration of single metal atoms, improves their stability, maximizes the effective active area, and significantly enhances their electrical conductivity and mass/electron transfer efficiency. These unique configurations bring M-SAAs outstanding electrochemical performance for DCE-EDR. The theoretical calculations also demonstrate that $\mathrm{\Delta\M-N_{4}}$ sites are responsible for the production of ethylene, while $\mathrm{M}{\mathrm{-N}_{3}}$ sites are beneficial for the formation of vinyl chloride. Particularly, $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}{-}\mathrm{N}_{3}$ sites exhibited the lowest energy barrier for ethylene and vinyl chloride, respectively. As a result, Cu-SAA with abundant $\mathrm{Cu-N_{4}}$ sites shows the optimal performance with the highest ethylene PR $(446~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ and FE $(64\%)$ reported thus far. Furthermore, this work provides a new strategy to exploit highly effective $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M-N-C}$ single-atom aerogel as self-supporting electrode and offers an intensive insight into the mechanism of electrochemical dechlorination reaction.
